{"id":"neet-2026-q113","pn":113,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Sickle-cell anaemia","q":"Which one of the following disorders is caused by the substitution of Glutamic acid (Glu) by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule?","o":["Haemophilia","Thalassemia","Sickle-cell anaemia","Phenylketonuria"],"c":2,"s":"Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosome-linked recessive trait.\nIt passes from parents to offspring when both partners are carriers of the gene.\nThe defect is caused by substitution of Glu by Val at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q114","pn":114,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Inheritance patterns","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Incomplete dominance — I. Human skin colour\nB. Co-dominance — II. Inheritance of flower colour in Antirrhinum sp.\nC. Pleiotropy — III. Phenylketonuria disease in humans\nD. Polygenic inheritance — IV. ABO blood groups\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"c":0,"s":"Flower colour in snapdragon (Antirrhinum sp.) is an example of incomplete dominance, so A-II.\nABO blood groups show co-dominance in individuals of AB group (IᴬIᴮ), so B-IV.\nPhenylketonuria is pleiotropy, since the gene responsible leads to multiple phenotypic effects, so C-III.\nHuman skin colour is controlled by three pairs of non-allelic genes, so it is polygenic inheritance, so D-I."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Sex determination in honeybees","q":"Which of the following statements are true with reference to the sex-determination in honeybees?\nA. An offspring formed from the union of a sperm and an egg, develops as a female (queen or worker).\nB. An unfertilized egg develops as a male by parthenogenesis.\nC. A male has half the number of chromosomes than that of a female.\nD. Males produce sperms by meiosis.\nE. Honeybees have a haplodiploid sex-determination system.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B, C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only","A, B, D and E only","A, B, C and E only"],"c":3,"s":"In honeybees, males have half the number of chromosomes of females.\nFemales are diploid with 32 chromosomes; males are haploid with 16 chromosomes.\nThis is the haplodiploid sex-determination system.\nA special feature is that males produce sperms by mitosis, not meiosis, so D is false.\nTrue statements: A, B, C and E."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q142","pn":142,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Mendel's monohybrid cross","q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : In an experiment, Mendel observed that the F1 progeny plants are all tall and none are dwarf.\nReason R : Stem height is a contrasting trait, with tall being dominant and dwarf being recessive.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],"c":3,"s":"Mendel crossed tall and dwarf plants and found all F₁ plants tall.\nThe tall trait (T) is dominant over the dwarf trait (t).\nBecause of this dominance, all F₁ plants are tall."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q130","pn":130,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Polygenic inheritance","q":"What is the pattern of inheritance for polygenic trait?","o":["Mendelian inheritance pattern","Non-mendelian inheritance pattern","Autosomal dominant pattern","X-linked recessive inheritance pattern"],"c":1,"s":"Polygenic inheritance means a trait is controlled by two or more genes.\nDisorders caused by mutation in a single gene pass on as per Mendelian principle.\nA polygenic trait shows a non-Mendelian inheritance pattern."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Test cross","q":"In a plant, black seed color (BB/Bb) is dominant over white seed color (bb). In order to find out the genotype of the black seed plant, with which of the following genotype will you cross it?","o":["Bb","BB/Bb","BB","bb"],"c":3,"s":"To find the genotype of a black-seeded plant, it is crossed with the white-seeded (homozygous recessive) plant.\nThis cross is called a test cross.\nSo the black plant (BB/Bb) must be crossed with bb."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q119","pn":119,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Incomplete dominance","q":"A pink flowered Snapdragon plant was crossed with a red flowered snapdragon plant. What type of phenotype/s is/are expected in the progeny?","o":["Only pink flowered plants","Red, pink as well as white flowered plants","Only red flowered plants","Red flowered as well as pink flowered plants"],"c":3,"s":"Pink snapdragon has genotype Rr.\nRed snapdragon has genotype RR.\nCross Rr x RR gives RR, RR, Rr, Rr.\nPhenotype ratio Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.\nSo only red and pink flowered plants are obtained.","sfig":{"w":380,"h":140,"alt":"Punnett square of Rr crossed with RR: top R, R; left R, r; cells RR, RR, Rr, Rr; phenotype Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.","d":[["r",2,8,234,126],["l",2,134,236,134],["l",2,126,2,134],["l",236,8,236,134],["l",2,8,82,78],["l",82,8,82,134],["l",142,8,142,134],["l",2,78,236,78],["l",2,106,236,106],["t",34,30,"♂"],["t",24,66,"♀"],["t",92,30,"R"],["t",152,30,"R"],["t",8,96,"R"],["t",92,96,"RR"],["t",152,96,"RR"],["t",8,124,"r"],["t",92,124,"Rr"],["t",152,124,"Rr"],["t",268,28,"Phenotype"],["t",268,96,"Red : Pink : White"],["t",268,122,"2"],["t",296,122,"2"],["t",330,122,"0"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q120","pn":120,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Genetic terms","q":"Mathch List I with List II\nList I:\n(A) Two or more alternative forms of a gene\n(B) Cross of F₁ progeny with homozygous recessive parent\n(C) Cross of F₁ progeny with any of the parents\n(D) Number of chromosome sets in plant\nList II:\n(I) Back cross\n(II) Ploidy\n(III) Allele\n(IV) Test cross\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"c":0,"s":"A. Alternative forms of a gene are alleles.\nB. F₁ crossed with the homozygous recessive parent is a test cross.\nC. F₁ crossed with either parent is a back cross.\nD. The number of chromosome sets in a plant is its ploidy.\nSo: A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q130","pn":130,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Law of dominance","q":"Which one of the following can be explained on the basis of Mendel's Law of Dominance?\n(A) Out of one pair of factors one is dominant and the other is recessive.\n(B) Alleles do not show any expression and both the characters appear as such in F₂ generation\n(C) Factors occur in pairs in normal diploid plants\n(D) The discrete unit controlling a particular character is called factor\n(E) The expression of only one of the parental characters is found in a monohybrid cross.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["B, C and D only","A, B, C, D and E","A, B and C only","A, C, D and E only"],"c":3,"s":"According to the Law of Dominance:\n(1) Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.\n(2) Factors occur in pairs.\n(3) In a dissimilar pair, one factor (dominant) masks the other (recessive).\nThis law explains why only one parental character is seen in a monohybrid cross.\nStatement B (alleles show no expression and both characters reappear in F₂) is explained by the Law of Segregation, not dominance.\nSo A, C, D and E are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q110","pn":110,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Pleiotropy","q":"The phenomenon of pleiotropism refers to -","o":["More than two genes affecting a single character","Presence of several alleles of a single gene controlling a single crossover","Presence of two alleles, each of the two genes controlling a single trait","A single gene affecting multiple phenotypic expression"],"c":3,"s":"When one gene affects several phenotypic traits, it is a pleiotropic gene.\nThis phenomenon is called pleiotropism."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q128","pn":128,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Linkage and gene mapping","q":"Frequency of recombination between gene pairs on same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes to map their position on chromosome, was used for the first time by","o":["Henking","Thomas Hunt Morgan","Sutton and Boveri","Alfred Sturtevant"],"c":3,"s":"Alfred Sturtevant used the recombination frequency between gene pairs on one chromosome as a measure of distance between them and so mapped their positions.\nSutton and Boveri gave the chromosomal theory of inheritance.\nHenking discovered the X-chromosome.\nMorgan proved the chromosomal theory and proposed linkage."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q116","pn":116,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Mendel's experiments","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Mendel studied seven pairs of contrasting traits in pea plants and proposed the Laws of Inheritance.\nStatement II : Seven characters examined by Mendel in his experiment on pea plants were seed shape and colour, flower colour, pod shape and colour, flower position and stem height.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":3,"s":"Mendel used 14 true-breeding varieties, which make seven contrasting pairs.\nThe traits: seed shape, seed colour, flower colour, pod shape, pod colour, flower position, stem height.\nBoth statements are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q143","pn":143,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Mendelian disorders","q":"Which of the following occurs due to the presence of autosome linked dominant trait?","o":["Myotonic dystrophy","Haemophilia","Thalessemia","Sickle cell anaemia"],"c":0,"s":"Haemophilia is X-linked recessive.\nThalassemia and sickle cell anaemia are autosomal recessive.\nMyotonic dystrophy is autosomal dominant, so it is due to an autosome linked dominant trait."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q149","pn":149,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Linkage and independent assortment","q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Mendel’s law of Independent assortment does not hold good for the genes that are located closely on the same chromosome.\nReason (R) : Closely located genes assort independently.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"c":1,"s":"Closely located genes do not assort independently, so (R) is wrong.\nIndependent assortment holds for genes on different chromosomes.\nIt fails for genes close together on one chromosome, so (A) is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q194","pn":194,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Linkage and gene mapping","q":"The recombination frequency between the genes a & c is 5%, b & c is 15%, b & d is 9%, a & b is 20%, c & d is 24% and a & d is 29%. What will be the sequence of these genes on a linear chromosome?","o":["d, b, a, c","a, b, c, d","a, c, b, d","a, d, b, c"],"c":2,"s":"1% recombination frequency = 1 centiMorgan, and the largest value marks the two end genes.\na–d is 29%, so a and d are the ends.\na–c is 5% so c is next to a; c–b is 15% and b–d is 9%.\nCheck: 5 + 15 + 9 = 29. So the order is a, c, b, d."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q195","pn":195,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Sex-linked inheritance","q":"If a colour blind female marries a man whose mother was colour blind, what are the chances of her progeny having color blindness?","o":["50%","75%","100%","25%"],"c":2,"s":"Colour blindness is X-linked recessive.\nThe man's mother is colour blind (XcXc), so he gets Xc from her and is colour blind (XcY).\nThe woman is colour blind (XcXc).\nAll children get Xc from the mother and Xc or Y from the father: sons XcY, daughters XcXc, so all are colour blind (100%)."}
